8508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15e89bf-dbe4-54e6-8d2f-e2da97637055" :question/math-academy-id "q-276787" :question/problem "Given the polynomial $P(x)=-x(x+3)(x-2)(x-4),$ find the solution of $P(x)<0.$\n\n$x\\in (\\,-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}$,\\,2)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $P(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=-x(x+3)(x-2)(x-4)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0,$ $x=2$ and $x=4,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a quartic polynomial with a negative leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/889f60e9a8dac71f99dd7e676b77c3e0a1eb7b944305e21b9d4d1b6897dbbb15.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)<0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)⋃(0,2)⋃(4,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality Containing No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879aa35a-b6ed-5487-817f-cfdd2d7df3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288512} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce0104d-ecd6-5db4-8aa4-ec358fd85e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ce0104d-ecd6-5db4-8aa4-ec358fd85e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288513} :answer-field/id #uuid "edfdcddc-3f2c-5359-be30-4fee91bff8aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fec0cc6f-044b-5b5d-a0a7-5c582639c70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec0cc6f-044b-5b5d-a0a7-5c582639c70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288515} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e00ead2-a557-5f1a-afd8-7f7882349b22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "110555f5-945b-5700-926f-9357921178e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-278843" :question/problem "Given $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x$, solve $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$ \n{{field-1}}\n$}\\,⋃\\,[$\n{{field-2}}$,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}+3x+3x+9) \\\\  & =x(x(x+3)+3(x+3)) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x+3) \\\\  & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x$ are $x=0$ and $x=-3,$ where\n\n- $x=-3$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=0$ is a simple root.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/24adb4d2477b6db5b51f538f5f3f57c197cbe3d1df53ad1b4e7e9479879f18dd.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in {-3}⋃[0,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality Containing No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e506e198-f2ce-554f-bc4b-1b135f2f23a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e506e198-f2ce-554f-bc4b-1b135f2f23a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288518} :answer-field/id #uuid "da6999a9-1465-5733-867f-f312828121a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cf0966-7b73-5013-9215-309042aa6e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03cf0966-7b73-5013-9215-309042aa6e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288520} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc369ce3-73f9-525a-8dbd-726cd2870b79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c48746af-85d0-5acc-af00-47481bc75b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48746af-85d0-5acc-af00-47481bc75b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288522} :answer-field/id #uuid "90f12b31-9b59-5842-870b-2a1e92a21c13" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3190673-507a-5810-b58d-da00a2c1d616" :question/math-academy-id "q-279291" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$⋃[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}[(x(x-1)-2(x-1)] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=0$, $x=1$ and $x=2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/baf599e732aa524409881d4bc7598c3f998cffe116ca1cdcd76fb6e6bd49a078.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in {0}⋃[1,2].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbff5997-1801-5dec-8e3d-e3d1ff309015" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality Using a Given Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cb15606-5092-51d1-8509-23aa2c1b265f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-3)∪(-2, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131547} {:answer/id #uuid "a2380419-7f42-5fd7-b9ae-1dae3926413c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-3)∪(-2, 1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131548} {:answer/id #uuid "bcba1809-8013-5327-865e-a1ff3fbd4526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-3)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131549} {:answer/id #uuid "d837645a-30fc-54a0-b9ea-b91ab6a8faf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131550} {:answer/id #uuid "3094926c-e366-57fd-aa55-d8b127b6c3da" 