-5b77-8112-b74cfae3d331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288527} {:answer/id #uuid "082836b6-734f-5344-8742-2512bc493410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288528} {:answer/id #uuid "a5125261-e7a9-5a8e-98d0-e134c04b1c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f1248f-676d-590a-8667-c07ed831bad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288526} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c6a6ef-4136-5c79-8544-4b1e3e5f79e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87cff0ed-70c3-5d25-98b9-ded71a3db91e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40188" :question/problem "Given the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)^{4}(x-1),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)^{4}(x-1)$ are $x=-3,x=0$ and $x=1,$ where\n\n- $x=-3$ has multiplicity $4,$ and\n\n- $x=0$ and $x=1$ are simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/6938cb283f25ccc325d7f50fcd793f1b9928025218bb6e9ca08f2d3941d16690.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/